mod.spybot
==========

spybot is a gnuworld client that watches the P10/P11 traffic its server sees
and reports it to an admin channel.  It does not scan or act on anything, it
only reports.

Reported to the admin channel (`adminchan`):

  * client connect, quit (with reason), kill, nick change
  * join, create, part, kick, topic
  * user mode and channel mode changes (OPMODE too), followed by the modes
    that are set afterwards, e.g. `sets mode: -i (+x)` or
    `sets mode: #chan +k key (+tnsk key)`.  Channel keys and limits are
    shown, A/U passes are only listed as letters.
  * server links and splits (squit), G-line set and removal

Nick, ident@host, IP, channel, server and modes each have their own colour
(`colour_nick`, `colour_userhost`, `colour_ip`, `colour_channel`,
`colour_server`, `colour_modes`; a mIRC colour 0-15, `fg,bg`, or `none`).
`colours = no` turns all colours off.

Debug channel
-------------
With `debugchan` set, every raw line read from or written to the uplink is
logged there as `[Sockread Server] -->` / `[Sockwrite Server] <--`.  Outgoing
lines come from the core event EVT_RAW_OUT (posted by xServer::writeLine), so
everything gnuworld sends is included, not just spybot's own output.
`debugignore` lists the P10/P11 commands left out (default `RI RO Z G`).
`/msg spybot DEBUG ON|OFF` toggles it at runtime.

Commands (IRC operators only, /msg spybot)
------------------------------------------
  IGNORE <server|client|channel> <mask>
  UNIGNORE <server|client|channel> <mask>
  LIST IGNORES [server|client|channel]
  DEBUG [ON|OFF]

Masks use wildcards.  Client masks are nick!user@host (host may be an IP);
a bare nick or user@host is expanded.  Ignoring a server also ignores its
clients.  Ignores are saved to `ignorefile` and loaded on startup.

Configuration
-------------
See bin/spybot.example.conf.  Config files must use LF line endings.

Build
-----
Enable with `./configure --enable-modules=...,spybot`, then load libspybot.
Needs EVT_RAW_OUT in include/events.h and src/server_connection.cc.
